    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d273-a17">

                <pb facs="0505" n="443" xml:id="pg443"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 17 in No.
                    40.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Carrié to Mr. Langston.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Office of the Secretary of State for the Department of Foreign
                                Affairs</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Port au
                            Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1878-04-01"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 1,
                    1878</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Minister</hi>: In maintaining as much as possible
                    its rights, its duties, and its prerogatives, as also its respect for its
                    official standing, my government has in no wise opposed the embarkation of the
                    refugees in your legation, yet it was its belief that said embarkation was to
                    have taken place on the 30th of March. With this in view, forces were held in
                    readiness, and the authorities only awaited your assistance in order to furnish
                    you its escort.</p>
                <p>In view of the amicable relations that my government desires to cultivate with
                    yours, through my medium, you are notified to cause to be embarked those
                    individuals who are a source of trouble to the country; and the government
                    cherishes the hope that you appreciate the case, which does not allow it to
                    consult the interest of those who are enemies of public peace.</p>
                <p>Knowing that in no wise would you assume the responsibility of keeping on the
                    territory of the republic persons who are a source of disquietude to society, I
                    have the honor to renew the assurances of my highest consideration.</p>
                <closer><signed><list>
                            <item>F. CARRIÉ.</item>
                            <item>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. M. Langston</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                    rendition="#italic">Minister Resident of the United States of
                                    America, Port au Prince.</hi></item>
                        </list></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
